      },
      {
        "row": 0,
        "col": 0,
        "sizeX": 0,
        "width": 12,
        "title": null,
        "hasTitle": false,
        "message": [
          "%md",
          "",
          "## Part 6 - Is there cycling infrastructure nearby?",
          "",
          "For every bicycle accident you measure the distance to the nearest cycling way. Then you summarise each DBSCAN cluster: how many accidents, how many serious ones, which accident type is most frequent, and which share happened within 25 m of a cycling way.",
          "",
          "###### How the nearest-neighbour search works",
          "",
          "**In one sentence:** for every accident, find the cycling way with the shortest distance to it.",
          "",
          "* **Distance from a point to a line:** the length of the shortest straight connection between the accident and any point of the cycling way, not just its start or end.",
          "* **Fast with a spatial index:** comparing every accident with every cycling way would mean 4,387 × 3,638, about 16 million distances. A spatial index sorts the geometries into nested boxes, like the sections of a map index. The search then only looks at cycling ways in the boxes close to the accident. GeoPandas (`sjoin_nearest`) and Oracle Spatial, which runs `nearest_neighbors()` on a table, both work this way.",
          "* **Why 25 m:** that is about the width of a main road including the pavements. Accident locations are recorded on the road, so a cycling way within 25 m runs along the same road or crosses it there."
        ],
        "selectedVisualization": "html",
        "visualizationConfig": null,
        "hideCode": true,
        "hideResult": false,
        "hideGutter": true,
        "hideVizConfig": true,
